---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Screenshot from "../components/Screenshot.astro";

// Issue #1105: "Import" — Clue, Apple Health, Health Connect, plus the
// round-trip restore of lunarlog's own export. You start the first import;
// the Clue import is a one-off file pick, while the Apple Health and Health
// Connect imports keep themselves current in the background once a profile
// is bound and the first import has completed (issue #993 — claimed below
// and in site/claims.md, checked by test/site/site_claims_test.dart).

const description =
  "Bring your history into lunarlog: import a Clue export file, read " +
  "Apple Health or Health Connect when you choose, or restore a lunarlog " +
  "export. Imports never overwrite what you logged by hand.";
---

<BaseLayout title="Import" description={description}>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Import</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      History is worth keeping when you move. lunarlog imports from Clue,
      from Apple Health on iPhone, and from Health Connect on Android. The
      first import is started by you, and the Clue import stays a one-off
      file pick; Apple Health and Health Connect imports then keep
      themselves current in the background once a profile is bound and
      that first import has completed — always labelled, and never
      written over something you logged by hand.
    </p>

    <figure class="screenshot-figure">
      <Screenshot
        screen="import"
        device="iphone-67"
        theme="light"
        loading="eager"
        alt="The import screen over a prepared import plan, showing what will be added."
      />
      <figcaption>The import screen, reviewing what an import will add.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h2>From Clue</h2>
      <!-- cite: lib/data/import/clue_importer.dart (rows stamped source 'clue_import' with checksum-derived source_id, so re-importing the same file is idempotent; issues #190/#199); provenance label in PRIVACY.md §2.A ("Imported from Clue"). -->
      <p>
        Export your data from Clue, pick the file in lunarlog, and review
        what the import will add before it runs. Every imported row is
        stamped as imported from Clue, so you can always tell imported
        history from logged history — and importing the same file twice
        adds nothing twice.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>From Apple Health or Health Connect</h2>
      <!-- cite: lib/data/health/health_import_service.dart (the first import is
           user-initiated; the whole available history read a page at a time, issue
           #992; on Android the read covers menstrual-flow AND intermenstrual-bleeding
           records — Health Connect's READ_MENSTRUATION and READ_INTERMENSTRUAL_BLEEDING,
           issue #1180 — and hand-logged values are never overwritten; own writes
           excluded by source; provenance stamped; "Bound. Two entry points." — since
           #993 the same import also runs prompt-free in the background, iOS observer +
           Android worker, #153 guard and the #1491 read-side probe inside importInBackground);
           PRIVACY.md §4 health paragraph. -->
      <p>
        On iPhone, lunarlog can read menstrual-flow history from Apple
        Health; on Android, flow and spotting history from Health Connect.
        You start the first import yourself from Settings; once a profile
        is bound and that first import has completed, the same import also
        keeps itself current in the background — Apple Health tells
        lunarlog when new data lands, and Android checks Health Connect on
        a schedule — under the same rules: no prompt, no screen,
        never a write, and it stops if you unbind the profile or revoke the
        permission. The read covers the whole history the health store
        holds, a page at a time, however far back it goes.
      </p>
      <p>
        What an import will and will not do:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A value you logged by hand is never overwritten — an imported
          value fills an empty day or refreshes a day that was itself
          imported.
        </li>
        <li>
          Entries lunarlog wrote to the health store itself are excluded, so
          a write-then-import round trip never doubles your history.
        </li>
        <li>
          Imported days are labelled with where they came from, on the same
          profile the device is bound to.
        </li>
        <li>
          The import is idempotent: if it is interrupted, run it again and
          nothing is duplicated.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §4 (the four Apple-computed cycle-deviation types read on iOS only, shown as a labelled, dismissible second opinion; never written back). -->
      <p>
        On iPhone, an import also surfaces Apple's own computed cycle
        deviations — irregular or infrequent cycles, prolonged periods,
        persistent intermenstrual bleeding — as a clearly labelled second
        opinion beside lunarlog's estimate. It is shown, never merged into
        your data, and never written back to Apple Health.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>From a lunarlog export</h2>
      <!-- cite: lib/domain/import/account_import.dart and lib/data/import/account_importer.dart (issue #140); additive merge — nothing overwritten or deleted (PRIVACY.md §7). -->
      <p>
        Moving to a new phone or back onto this one: a JSON file you
        exported from lunarlog can be read back in. The restore adds what
        is missing and overwrites nothing — merging, not replacing — and
        works without an account.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What stays put</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §2.A storage paragraph ("before you sign in, none of it leaves the device") and §1 "Resilient Offline". -->
      <p>
        Importing works on the copy of your data on this device. Nothing
        leaves the device unless you sign in and turn on sync — the same
        rule every other write follows.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="doc__footer-note">
      Getting data out instead? <a href="/export">Export</a> covers JSON,
      CSV, FHIR, and the clinician PDF.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
